<p>Tarik Skubal has been one of baseball&#8217;s best pitchers this season, and tonight he gets to prove it against a Cincinnati lineup that has quietly become one of the league&#8217;s most vulnerable against elite velocity. The Dodgers&#8217; right-hander is sitting at 2.86 ERA with a 0.98 WHIP across 132 innings—that&#8217;s a pitcher who doesn&#8217;t miss. Nick Lodolo, meanwhile, has been treading water at 5.08 with a swollen WHIP, and while the Reds&#8217; left-hander can touch 97 and find the zone, he&#8217;s been doing neither consistently enough to match what Skubal brings. This is a matchup where one team&#8217;s ace genuinely outclasses the other&#8217;s, and that matters in September when every win carries weight.</p>
<p>Cincinnati comes in needing this game more than Los Angeles, which alone tells you something about how the pitching dynamic is going to play out. When your starter is getting hit around and the other guy is pinpoint efficient, you&#8217;re playing from behind before the first pitch lands. But the Reds aren&#8217;t here just to make up the numbers—they have weapons that can disrupt things if Skubal has an off night.</p>
<h2>Cincinnati Reds Players to Watch</h2>
<p>Elly De La Cruz remains the engine here, and his season-long profile of .281 average with 25 stolen bases is exactly the kind of pressure point that matters against a pitcher like Skubal, who typically works at a brisk pace. The concern for Cincinnati is that De La Cruz has a tendency to expand his zone against hard throwers, and Skubal&#8217;s fastball sits in that 96-97 range where it looks like a strike from the box even when it&#8217;s not. If De La Cruz gets ahead early—and this is critical—he&#8217;s the kind of guy who can steal second and third and create chaos. If he falls behind, he becomes a strikeout candidate. Look for his strikeout prop to be vulnerable if Skubal finds his rhythm early.</p>
<p>Sal Stewart is having a genuinely elite season with 31 home runs and 107 RBI in 144 games, but the hard truth is that his .266 average masks a guy who struggles more often than you&#8217;d think against upper-90s heat. Skubal&#8217;s slider has a chase rate that would make any pitcher smile, and Stewart swings at things. The over on his strikeouts tonight should be tempting—he&#8217;s the kind of power hitter who can go 0-for-4 with two strikeouts and still feel like he almost did something because he hit one hard foul. Against Skubal, the strikeouts come before the hero swings.</p>
<h2>Los Angeles Dodgers Players to Watch</h2>
<p>Freddie Freeman hitting .299 with an .835 OPS over 139 games is the kind of consistency that doesn&#8217;t always show up in the flashy headlines, but it absolutely shows up in the box score. Against Lodolo specifically, Freeman&#8217;s approach is going to be crucial because the Reds&#8217; pitcher has been spotty with his command all season. Freeman doesn&#8217;t chase much, and when a pitcher is struggling with location the way Lodolo has been, Freeman gets to hunt. His doubles total on the season doesn&#8217;t jump out at you until you realize he&#8217;s in position to extend it significantly tonight if Lodolo can&#8217;t pound the zone. The hits over prop here could have real appeal given that Lodolo is going to miss, Freeman is going to wait, and contact is probably coming.</p>
<p>Max Muncy&#8217;s .839 OPS with 26 home runs speaks to a guy who still has power equity in his swing even as he&#8217;s entered his mid-thirties. The angle isn&#8217;t complicated: Lodolo&#8217;s fastball has been getting hammered this season, and Muncy&#8217;s home run rate against left-handed pitchers sits higher than his overall number suggests. If Lodolo stays out of the zone and falls behind, Muncy gets a fastball he can hurt. Target his slugging line with confidence.</p>
