<p>Quinn Mathews is about to walk into Oracle Park with a 1.58 WHIP and a Cardinals bullpen that has already surrendered 1.47 baserunners per nine innings this month. That&#8217;s not a preview. That&#8217;s a problem waiting to happen. The Giants are bringing a league-average hitter&#8217;s park and a pitcher in Landen Roupp who, for all his 8-13 record and inflated ERA, has actually been stingy with free passes — 1.29 WHIP over 144 innings is the kind of control metric that survives September collapses. What matters tonight is not whether Roupp is an ace. It&#8217;s whether Mathews can survive five innings without coughing up the kind of extra baserunners that turn close games into laughers.</p>
<p>The Cardinals sent their fourth starter to San Francisco during a stretch where Jordan Walker and Alec Burleson have been punishing any pitcher who leaves the ball in the zone. Walker&#8217;s .284 average looks modest until you remember he&#8217;s 28 home runs and 100 RBIs through 141 games — he&#8217;s been the engine of this offense all season, and he&#8217;s the kind of hitter who doesn&#8217;t care about pitcher names or ERA. Put the ball anywhere near the middle of the plate, and he&#8217;s not fooling himself into taking a called strike. Burleson has been just as relentless, sitting at .283 with 104 RBIs, and together they represent the one Cardinal lineup advantage that actually translates to diamond advantage: disciplined, productive righties who will make Mathews work.</p>
